# Grant /dev/uinput access to the active local session user so that
# Plumed can open it directly to create a virtual keyboard for
# Wayland typing and paste injection (Ctrl+V, Ctrl+C, Enter).
#
# systemd-logind applies an ACL to devices tagged `uaccess` for the user of
# the currently active local seat, so only that user gets access: not SSH
# sessions, background services, other logged-in users, nor members of a
# group. The node stays owned by root:root; MODE 0660 keeps the group bits
# (the ACL mask) open so the logind ACL entry grants read and write, while
# the root group itself only holds root.
#
# Not shipped in the packages: it is installed only when the user runs the
# command shown by the app when direct typing on Wayland is unavailable
# (the "Copy command" button of the `wayland-inject-unavailable` toast,
# `src/features/layout/listeners/wayland-listener.tsx`, which builds the
# command from this file's rule lines, so this file is the one copy).
#
# Security scope: this grants *write* access to uinput (input injection),
# NOT read access to /dev/input/event* (keylogging). A malicious program of
# the active user could synthesise keystrokes but cannot observe them.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput", MODE="0660", TAG+="uaccess"
